

---

1. Key Improvements

1. Autoencoder Anomaly Scoring

After training, the autoencoder can predict (reconstruct) new bump patches.

We calculate the mean absolute error (or MSE) between the input and reconstructed patch.

The higher the error, the more likely the bump is anomalous.



2. Optional Autoencoder-Based Defect Detection

In the “Detect Bumps” phase, if the user enables “Use Autoencoder for anomaly detection,” each detected bump is passed through the autoencoder to compute an anomaly score.

If the score exceeds a user-defined threshold, the bump is flagged as defective.



3. Updating (Retraining/Fine-Tuning) the Model

If new data is available (e.g., new good bumps or newly identified anomalies), you can load the existing autoencoder weights and call fit again to continue training (fine-tune) on the new data, or retrain from scratch as needed.

Keras doesn’t allow partial fitting in the sense of incremental batch updates over time, but you can replicate a partial approach by re-calling fit with your new dataset (plus optionally some old data).





