3. Using the Autoencoder Anomaly Scores (How It Works)

1. Train the Autoencoder

Click “Train Autoencoder” after detecting bumps you believe to be good (or mostly good).

The autoencoder will learn to reconstruct these normal bumps.



2. Set “Use AE Anomaly” to True

In the Recipe Settings, check “Use AE Anomaly” and set an anomaly threshold (e.g., 0.05).

The threshold is empirical—you adjust it based on your data distribution (view reconstruction errors of good vs. defective bumps).



3. Detect Bumps

The code automatically predicts each bump patch, calculates the reconstruction error, and if the error > threshold, it’s flagged in red as an anomaly.



4. Update / Fine-Tune

If you gather new, reliable data (good or mislabeled bumps), you can load them using “Update Model”.

The code will continue training (fit again) on this new data, effectively fine-tuning the autoencoder.





---

4. How to Update the Model

Retraining vs. Fine-Tuning

1. Fine-Tuning

Load the existing autoencoder (with already trained weights).

Call model.fit(new_data, new_data) to continue training.

Good if new data is similar in distribution but adds a bit more variety.



2. Full Retraining

If a large amount of new data or new defect classes appear, you might want to:

1. Combine old + new data into a single dataset.


2. Re-initialize or load the old model.


3. Train from scratch or from old weights.





3. Practical Considerations

Keep an archive of “good” bumps.

Periodically re-validate your threshold.

Save your model weights (model.save("autoencoder.h5")) so you can revert if needed.





---

5. Recommendations & Further Steps

1. Better Architecture

For subtle defects, consider deeper or specialized networks (e.g., U-Net-style autoencoders).

Use SSIM loss or perceptual losses for improved reconstructions.



2. Outlier Score Calibration

Plot distribution of reconstruction errors for a known set of good bumps and some known defective bumps.

Pick a threshold that yields a low false positive rate but still catches real defects.



3. Integration with YOLO or Other Detectors

If you prefer bounding-box detection for multiple defect classes, YOLO can be trained to detect missing, bridging, or large voids directly.



4. Real-Time Monitoring

Move from Tkinter to an industrial GUI or a web-based front-end for remote monitoring.

Integrate with a database to store inspection results over time for big data analysis.



